LIMINA A/B lever: rebuild the glyph primitive on every draw.

Against mutter 50.0's clutter/clutter/pango/clutter-pango-display-list.c. Built and installed into
the guest with install-mutter-50.sh; see RESULTS.md for what the arm measured.

The cached glyph VBO is the one structural difference between this path and the cogl journal, which
rebuilds its buffer every frame and never loses content on the virgl/vrend path. Dropping the re-use
tests whether the cached buffer's contents reach the host. It is NOT a fix -- it discards the
optimisation this code exists for, and it churns a GL buffer object per glyph run.

--- a/clutter/clutter/pango/clutter-pango-display-list.c
+++ b/clutter/clutter/pango/clutter-pango-display-list.c
@@ -267,6 +267,29 @@
    * that if the text doesn't change from frame to frame the VBO can
    * be re-used avoiding the repeated cost of validating the data and
    * mapping it into the GPU... */
 
+  /* LIMINA A/B lever, LIMINA_TEXT_NOCACHE=1: rebuild the glyph primitive (and its VBO) on every
+   * draw instead of re-using the cached one.
+   *
+   * The cache is the one structural difference between this path and the cogl journal, which
+   * rebuilds its buffer every frame and never loses content on the virgl/vrend path. If dropping
+   * the re-use restores the text, the fault is the cached buffer's contents not reaching the host,
+   * not anything about the draw itself. Not a fix: it discards the optimisation this code exists
+   * for. */
+  {
+    static int nocache = -1;
+    if (nocache < 0)
+      {
+        nocache = g_getenv ("LIMINA_TEXT_NOCACHE") != NULL;
+        g_printerr ("[LIMINA] clutter glyph primitive cache %s\n",
+                    nocache ? "DISABLED (LIMINA_TEXT_NOCACHE)" : "on (default)");
+      }
+    if (nocache && node->d.texture.primitive != NULL)
+      {
+        g_object_unref (node->d.texture.primitive);
+        node->d.texture.primitive = NULL;
+      }
+  }
+
   if (node->d.texture.primitive == NULL)
     {
       CoglAttributeBuffer *buffer;
